A||N||9||862M4inCA||Headlight switch problem???||||9||
Z||000000||862M4inCA||12-26-1999||02:43 AM||||Tonight while driving home for a friends house I was using my high beams down a pooly lit road when all of a sudden everything went black. All I had was my parking lights. The high beam indicator went dead and I tried switching back to low beams but still nothing. I turned the light switch off and back on a couple times and they finally came back on. I've never had a problem like this when using only low beams, just my highs. This has happened a couple other times before and the last time it happened it took a couple minutes for the lights to come back on. Is this a headlight switch problem or should I look elsewhere? Please help!    ||||reg||9
Z||000001||Luperman||12-26-1999||03:18 AM||||sounds like it would be your headlight switch maybe a loose conection on the back side of the switch could be rattling loose.||||reg||10
Z||000002||862M4inCA||12-26-1999||01:11 PM||||I checked for loose wires, but I'm just thinking that if my switch itself was bad, wouldn't I still have the same problems with low beams as I do with highs? Also it doesn't make a difference if I'm driving or just sitting in the driveway. Is there some kinda of circuit breaker for the headlight system that isn't located on the fuse block? If there is one, could someone tell me where to look and how to check that sucker. I would really like to have high beams without the fear that I'm going to loose all my lights while driving. Thanx in advance for any info. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||TOM||12-26-1999||02:04 PM||||I think the breaker is in the switch. On high they draw more amps and thus could trip a faulty breaker.||||reg||1
Z||000004||862M4inCA||12-27-1999||07:34 PM||||Does anyone know of a way I can test the breaker to be certain that it IS faulty? If not I guess I'll be planning a trip to the boneyard and see about pulling a "new" switch and see if that helps. Thanx for the feedback guys. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Sage||12-27-1999||07:47 PM||||Most automotive breakers reset when you unplug them, then plug them back in.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Uaana||12-28-1999||12:09 AM||||I just had the same prob myself. <BR>Replace your headlight switch 19.96us<BR>at any GM dealer. Works great now!<BR>Only drawback is the new replacement part<BR>is no longer 2 tone grey/black it only<BR>comes in mono black. Hope this helps.||||reg||14
Z||000007||dogtired||12-28-1999||12:40 AM||||Tried to E-Mail you direct from here but it didn't work? Must be this boat anchor I am using?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"><BR>Anyway I have an extra 86 headlight switch assembly, used but I know its good.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR> I would take 1/2 what a new one is going for? If interested let me know.||||reg||6
Z||000008||862M4inCA||12-28-1999||12:40 PM||||Well guys, I appreciate all the feedback. I'm planning on taking a trip to the wrecking yard today to see about picking up another switch. Might get two just in case one of them is dead or almost dead. I don't think I can go very wrong for only $3 each. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Monkeyman||12-28-1999||11:23 PM||||Sometimes you get what you pay for.  If you find that the switch is the problem, maybe you should spring for a new switch (to avoid the same problem in a few days/weeks/whatever) and save the "good" junkyard switch for an emergency.  (Maybe in the trunk?)  You never know when the new used switch will go out.  (Is there such a thing as a new, used anything?  Am I making sense?  Do I really exist?  I think, therefore I am.  Or is it, "I'm pink, therefore I'm Spam"?  I forget.)||||reg||1
